From 569e238fece690fa94b705cc26d6e97e9ecf52c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Olof Dahlbom <933408+olofd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:57:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] touchbar: sleep until input instead of polling; level sliders

The built-in renderer's loop polled the hardware socket every 5 ms, waking
the CPU about 200 times a second while the bar sat idle. It now sleeps in
poll(2) until a packet arrives, its next timer is due (overlay fade,
enrollment progress, hold-to-repeat) or a background thread has news: the
desktop provider and overlay watcher wake it through a local socket pair.
Idle, the main thread wakes once a second (the provider's status).
t1_seqpacket_wait_readable() and SeqPacketClient::wait_readable() add the
blocking wait; without a wake channel the loop keeps its old polling.

Sliding a finger on the volume, display brightness or keyboard backlight
controls turns the bar into one wide slider for that level, which follows
the finger relative to where it started and stays 1.5 s after it lifts.
Volume goes through the desktop provider as before; brightness and
backlight use SetDisplayBrightness / SetKeyboardBacklight, one request at
a time with only the newest level waiting, and the level popup is shown
once after the last. The starting level is read with
SystemBrightnessControls::current_percentage().

Tested on a MacBookPro13,2 (t1bridge 0.1.12, Omarchy, kernel 7.2.5).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
